this is all that remains of  eight-year-old hannah's house  she lives in the neighborhood of  bobrawicza on the outskirts of chernhiv  there is no water electricity and gas no  one comes here and asks no one does  anything  she's not the only one to have lost her  home  her neighbor alexei serrada shows us  what was once his pride and joy  all that's left are the ashes of a  previous life  his home was first hit by russian bombs  in the early days of the war and again  on the 10th of march  now i have nothing the ceiling and  floors are burnt the most painful thing  is that all my memorable documents have  burnt  i didn't need them but i kept them as  souvenirs i'm a retired pilot and i kept  all my pilot logs there  despite having lost all his possessions  he looks to the future with hope  come back in two years i will rebuild  everything we will have a barbecue  together  chernhee woke up to the rocket attacks  on the first day of the war when russian  troops crossed the border from belarus  despite being besieged it did not  surrender and finally on the 30th of  march the russian forces retreated from  the region  but chernhee has paid a high price  andriy and svetlana's home was hit three  times and now they are trying to salvage  whatever remains  you see how our russian brothers have  helped us  we met hannah again in a nearby shelter  they have shown me my room already it is  a nice room i will stay here as long as  i can i don't have a choice  our center was created for children with  psycho-neurological difficulties when  the bombing started many people came  here as they were looking for shelter we  became a shelter for those who needed it  for hannah it might not be an ideal  setup but at least she now has a roof  over her head  not only this rehab center for children  has changed after the war in ukraine  civilians are now becoming frontline  workers helping to rebuild the country  and they say in this process they are  more united than ever resourcer al  jazeera chernham
 

